<h2>A three-month marriage and a dismissed claim</h2>
<p>The parties married on 9 March 2023 under Hindu rites and separated roughly three months later. The wife claimed Rs. 15,000 per month under Section 144 BNSS, alleging the husband earned about Rs. 50,000 from salary, land, a pig farm and a goods vehicle. The Principal Judge, Family Court, Nalbari (Case No. 82/2025) dismissed her claim on 18 May 2026, influenced by two strands: her social media posts suggesting an enjoyable life in Guwahati, and her voluntary departure from the matrimonial home.</p>

<h2>What the High Court corrected</h2>
<p>The Division Bench identified the central error: treating curated online imagery as proof of earning capacity. The posts, it held, did not <em>&ldquo;prove that the petitioner-wife was able to finance her lifestyle through employment.&rdquo;</em> Her affidavit and an employer&rsquo;s certificate established actual earnings of Rs. 7,500 per month as a sales promoter — enough to maintain herself only in part.</p>
<p>On the husband&rsquo;s side, the Court declined to accept his disclosed salary of about Rs. 14,000 at face value. His loan repayments and dealings around a goods vehicle did not square with that figure, and the Court took his income from all sources at Rs. 18,000 per month — while finding the wife&rsquo;s Rs. 50,000 estimate unsupported. Neither side proved its allegations of infidelity against the other, and no divorce or restitution petition was pending. The result: the Family Court&rsquo;s order was set aside and the husband was directed to pay Rs. 2,000 per month from November 2026, by the 7th of each month. The Bench grounded the award in first principles, observing that <em>&ldquo;it is the duty of the husband to maintain his wife&rdquo;</em> and that <em>&ldquo;the object is to prevent vagrancy and destitution.&rdquo;</em></p>

<h2>How courts read means and lifestyle</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Material</th><th>What it can show</th><th>What it cannot show</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Social media posts</td><td>Lifestyle, travel, associations — context that may invite scrutiny</td><td>Actual income or the means to sustain the depicted lifestyle</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Salary disclosure</td><td>A floor for assessment, if consistent with spending</td><td>Full capacity, where loans, vehicles or businesses tell a different story</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Employer&rsquo;s certificate</td><td>The claimant&rsquo;s actual earnings and the shortfall to be bridged</td><td>Disentitlement — partial earnings reduce, not erase, the claim</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Loan repayments and assets</td><td>A basis to assess real income from all sources</td><td>A priority over the maintenance obligation itself</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why the decision matters in practice</h2>
<p>Screenshots of a claimant&rsquo;s social media are now a near-standard annexure in maintenance oppositions. This judgment draws the line that matters: such material may justify questions, but it is not a substitute for proof of income. Equally, the decision illustrates the two-sided discipline of disclosure that governs maintenance adjudication — the claimant&rsquo;s actual earnings were counted against her claim, and the respondent&rsquo;s understated salary was corrected against his own financial conduct. Both sides stand or fall on verifiable means, not on narrative.</p>
<div class="note">Maintenance under Section 144 BNSS is a summary remedy; findings on income and conduct at this stage do not decide title, matrimonial fault or final rights in divorce proceedings. Figures in this article are those recorded in the judgment and are case-specific, not benchmarks.</div>
